  "style": {
    "id": "ukiyo-e",
    "name": "Ukiyo-e",
    "description": "Japanese woodblock printing in passes: a sheet of cream washi with kozo fibres lies on a pale hinoki bench against two wooden kento registration guides. Carved cherry-wood blocks swing down onto it one at a time like a lid, press (the key block gets a baren rubbing pass over its back), and swing up again to reveal the ONE flat layer each has left in register: first the black key line, then the sky bokashi gradation, Prussian blue, a second blue, ochre and pink, until an original great-wave-and-mountain seascape is complete; a red seal stamps it and the printed layers lift a few centimetres off the paper for a gentle parallax. Each block's underside carries its own inked relief (mirror-correct when it opens) and kento marks. Layers are multiplied onto the lit paper like transparent ink, so fibre and wood grain show in every flat colour and the key line survives every pass.",
